\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces Audit design: a fixed set of pose outputs is scored under two contrast axes. (a) Data and provenance: the official PHOENIX-2014T split (7{,}096/519/642) was materialized by the SLRTP2025 release as 7{,}060/515/641 poses, matching the released evaluator's training manifest (36/4/1 official records absent). (b) Fixed output conditions for one German source sentence: recorded test poses (REC), Progressive-Transformer output (PT), whole-donor copy TN-PURE (source-text token-set Jaccard, exact-normalized-text exclusion), and PT-scaffold composition TN-PTCOMP. (c) Two contrast axes: the evaluator (released, fourteen recipe reconstructions, heterogeneous diagnostic checkpoints) and the reference frame (speech-transcribed vs.\ human sign-to-text), with pose outputs and the donor registry fixed; the primary estimand is $\Delta _c=\mathrm  {BLEU}_c(\mathrm  {PURE})-\mathrm  {BLEU}_c(\mathrm  {REC})$.\relax }}{17}{figure.caption.97}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:s4_audit_design}{{S4}{17}{Audit design: a fixed set of pose outputs is scored under two contrast axes. (a) Data and provenance: the official PHOENIX-2014T split (7{,}096/519/642) was materialized by the SLRTP2025 release as 7{,}060/515/641 poses, matching the released evaluator's training manifest (36/4/1 official records absent). (b) Fixed output conditions for one German source sentence: recorded test poses (REC), Progressive-Transformer output (PT), whole-donor copy TN-PURE (source-text token-set Jaccard, exact-normalized-text exclusion), and PT-scaffold composition TN-PTCOMP. (c) Two contrast axes: the evaluator (released, fourteen recipe reconstructions, heterogeneous diagnostic checkpoints) and the reference frame (speech-transcribed vs.\ human sign-to-text), with pose outputs and the donor registry fixed; the primary estimand is $\Delta _c=\mathrm {BLEU}_c(\mathrm {PURE})-\mathrm {BLEU}_c(\mathrm {REC})$.\relax }{figure.caption.97}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Small-sample overfit test.}{17}{section*.140}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces Human sign-to-text references remove most of the replay advantage on the matched high-confidence subset ($n=461$; released evaluator, canonical hypotheses). (a) Corpus BLEU-4 under original vs.\ human references: REC 14.94 $\to $ 7.35, PURE 24.98 $\to $ 8.30; the reference-frame contrast reduces the gap $+10.03 \to +0.95$ (90.5\%). (b) Bootstrap distribution of the contrast (10{,}000 paired resamples; mean 9.08, 95\% CI $[7.44, 10.86]$). (c) PURE$-$REC gap under original vs.\ human references across BLEU-4, BLEU-1, and chrF. References are back-translations by a single deaf fluent L2 DGS signer\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {czehmann2026sacred}; replacement cannot distinguish reference-text defects, paraphrase shift, BLEU floor effects, or disruption of the lexical coupling.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.98}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:s5_ref_frame}{{S5}{18}{Human sign-to-text references remove most of the replay advantage on the matched high-confidence subset ($n=461$; released evaluator, canonical hypotheses). (a) Corpus BLEU-4 under original vs.\ human references: REC 14.94 $\to $ 7.35, PURE 24.98 $\to $ 8.30; the reference-frame contrast reduces the gap $+10.03 \to +0.95$ (90.5\%). (b) Bootstrap distribution of the contrast (10{,}000 paired resamples; mean 9.08, 95\% CI $[7.44, 10.86]$). (c) PURE$-$REC gap under original vs.\ human references across BLEU-4, BLEU-1, and chrF. References are back-translations by a single deaf fluent L2 DGS signer~\cite {czehmann2026sacred}; replacement cannot distinguish reference-text defects, paraphrase shift, BLEU floor effects, or disruption of the lexical coupling.\relax }{figure.caption.98}{}}
